{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And it was in those many days.</b> When the days that passed grew long — four hundred years from the time of the covenant between the pieces — and they supposed that the appointed end of the servitude had passed." "<b>Those many.</b> In a time of distress they are called 'many,' and in a time of joy they are called 'few.' As it is written: ״ויהיו בעיניו כימים אחדים באהבתו אותה״ (and they were in his eyes like a few days, because of his love for her). The meaning: after they had passed, the entire servitude had been only brief." "<b>And the king of Egypt died.</b> The Holy One, blessed be He, hastened to bring about the death of the king of Egypt, and afterward ״ויאמר ה׳ אל משה במדין לך שוב מצרים כי מתו כל האנשים המבקשים את נפשך״ — lest Moses say: how can I go, when Pharaoh will hear and kill me? As it is written: ״ויבקש להרוג את משה״. And even without this, he would have made excuses, as it is written: ״מי אנכי כי אלך אל פרעה״, and he says: ״לא איש דברים אנכי״, and he says: ״שלח נא ביד תשלח״." "<b>And the children of Israel sighed.</b> They knew that a servitude of four hundred years had been imposed upon them, but they supposed it had begun from the covenant between the pieces, and therefore <b>they sighed</b>. And on account of this, the children of Ephraim hastened to leave — but it had begun only from when Isaac was born. Another interpretation: as long as that king was alive, they harbored hope that perhaps when he died, his decrees would be annulled, for so is the custom: when a king dies, all the prisoners in every city are immediately released. But when this one died and his decrees were not annulled, they said: from now on there is no end to the matter — therefore ״ויאנחו״."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:55:58.458173295Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}